; A bounded pool of concurrent tasks, joined in SUBMIT order, built on
; the primitives `(parallel ...)` desugars to.
;
; This replaces `stdlib/Job.ax`, which was a pool of child PROGRAMS over
; `sysSpawn`/`sysWaitPid`. The replacement is strictly more general:
; `parRunAll` is `parMapWords` over a closure that runs one argv, and
; `parMapWords` will run any Axiom thunk. `docs/memory-model.md`
; MM-PAR-2 named "`Job` cannot run an Axiom closure - it execs an
; external program" as Job's hard limit; that limit is lifted here.
;
; WHY `__proc_spawn` AND NOT `__par_spawn`, and why that is not a
; concession to AX3064.
;
; `parMapWords` hands the caller's thunk to a spawn, and that thunk
; captures whatever the caller wrote - a `Vec` of argv vectors, in
; `parRunAll`'s case. AX3064 (`capSpawnHead`, self_host/typecheck.ax)
; refuses a captured reference at `__par_spawn` and `__thread_spawn`,
; because those two may lower to a pthread SHARING the parent's address
; space, where a non-atomic refcount is a race (MM-PAR-6).
;
; `__proc_spawn` NAMES the forked lowering. Its isolation is MM-PAR-3's
; by construction - the child gets a copy-on-write address space, so a
; captured reference is the child's own - and `capSpawnHead`'s own
; header says the exemption is a statement about that primitive rather
; than about a build flag. So this module does not weaken, dodge or
; argue with the rule: it names the lowering whose guarantee it needs.
;
; AND WHAT AX3064 SEES OF THIS MODULE'S CALLERS, which is the part
; a reader has to be told rather than left to assume.
;
; `checkSpawnCaptures` scans a thunk that is LITERALLY a lambda, and
; refuses a thunk that is a frame-local name of arrow type - a
; parameter, a `let`-bound name - because its captures are not visible
; where it stands (`tests/diagnostics/643-parallel-capture-hop.ax`).
; Had this module named `__par_spawn`, the spawn of `f` below would
; draw AX3064 for every caller, capturing or not: a parameter's
; captures cannot be proven absent. It names `__proc_spawn`, whose
; forked lowering isolates by construction, so the exemption above
; applies and callers may capture freely. The two halves meet at one
; fact - this file names `__proc_spawn` - which is why §6b gates the
; primitive choice rather than commenting it.
;
; What it means HERE is that the safety of every caller's capture rests
; on ONE fact: this file names `__proc_spawn`. Nothing in the type
; system will notice if that word changes. So the fact is GATED rather
; than commented - `scripts/check-parallel.sh` section 6 arm (b) builds
; this pool with `--threads` and without and requires the two modules to
; be BYTE-IDENTICAL, which they are (measured: 0 pthread declares and 0
; `thread_local` lines out of both). Ablated - `__proc_spawn` swapped
; for `__par_spawn` in a copy of `stdlib/` - the `--threads` build grows
; 2 pthread declares and 7 thread-local globals and the arm goes red.
; That gate is standing in for the check the rule cannot make.
;
; WHY THE RESULT ORDER IS THE SUBMIT ORDER, ALWAYS. Inherited from
; `Job.ax` unchanged, for the reason it gave: a pool that answered in
; completion order is a pool whose output depends on which core was
; free, and this project compares bytes for a living. `__axiom_par_join_proc`
; is a `wait4` on a SPECIFIC pid, not a `wait` for any child, which is
; what lets "join the oldest outstanding" bound the width without
; reordering anything. Completion order is not exposed; there is
; deliberately no API for it. That is MM-PAR-5.
;
; WIDTH BOUNDS RUNNING TASKS, NOT SUBMISSIONS. The submit loop blocks
; by joining the oldest outstanding handle whenever `width` are already
; out, so a thousand-case sweep never forks a thousand processes. The
; width is CLAMPED to at least 1 rather than trusted: a width of 0
; would never spawn and never finish.
;
; THE ONE BEHAVIOUR THAT DIFFERS FROM `Job`, and a caller must know it.
; A thunk that TRAPS propagates its status in the PARENT.
; `__axiom_par_join_proc` re-raises a non-zero wait status through the
; parent's recovery point, or exits with it when none is armed. A spawn
; the kernel refuses, or one the handle table has no slot for, raises
; 78 the same way. The runtime sweeps outstanding children on that path
; - MM-PAR-7 SIGKILLs and reaps every child spawned since the recovery
; point was armed, this pool's included - and the pool holds no mapping,
; so what the abort skips is its arena storage, which the recovery
; point's reset reclaims with everything else above its mark.
; `scripts/check-task.sh` §9 records the pids and requires them gone.
;
; This does NOT affect `parRunAll`'s contract. An external program that
; fails - exits 42, dies of a signal, or does not exist at all - is not
; a trap: `parRunOne` answers a word and its child exits 0. Only an
; Axiom-side trap inside the thunk propagates.
;
; PER-SLOT FAILURE OF THOSE TRAPS is `parMapWordsChecked`'s, below. It
; joins through `__proc_join_nr` - the join that stores the decoded
; wait status to an out-cell instead of re-raising it - and answers a
; `Vec` of `Result`: `Ok` the thunk's word, `Err` the slot's wait
; status (an exit code, or 128 plus the signal) as an `Error` code.
; The trap's own message still goes to fd 2 - the child writes it as
; it dies, and no join can unwrite that - but the parent survives, the
; remaining slots still join, and the answer is in submit order like
; every other answer this module gives. `parMapWords` keeps the
; raising join: a checked pool is a choice the caller makes by naming
; it, not a behaviour change to the pool that exists.
;
; THE ERROR CONTRACT IS `sysRun`'S, EXACTLY, unchanged from `Job`:
;
;   >= 0   the child's exit code
;   128+n  the child was killed by signal n
;   < 0    the spawn's own -errno: the program NEVER RAN
;
; That last distinction is the one a driver must not lose - "could not
; start llc" and "llc rejected the module" are different failures.
(import Mem)
(import Str)
(import Vec)
(import Sys)
(import Err)

; Run `f i` for every `i` in `0 .. n`, at most `width` at once,
; answering the results in SUBMIT order.
;
; `hs` is a ring with at most min(n, w) slots, each a `Spawn`: the
; handle a spawn answers and its join takes (docs/memory-model.md
; MM-PAR-8), so a slot joined twice would trap with status 85 rather
; than read an unmapped page. Slot i % w is reused only after the old
; child in it has joined; `joined` counts submitted results, not
; positions in the ring. `out` still holds n results, so
; total result storage is O(n), while handle storage is O(min(n, w)).
; Capacity is capped by n before allocation: a huge width over a short
; or empty input does not ask for a huge ring. Vec clamps an empty or
; negative capacity to one word.
(pub :: parMapWords (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (parMapWords f n width)
  (let (
    (w (if (< width 1)
      1
      width))
    (hs (vecWithCapacity
      (if (< n w)
        n
        w)))
    (out vecNew)
    (mut joined 0)
  )
    {
      (for i in 0..n
        {
          ; Block before spawning, not after, so at most `w` children
          ; are ever outstanding. `(- i joined)` is the count of
          ; handles spawned but not yet joined.
          (while (>= (- i joined) w)
            {
              (vecPush out (__proc_join (vecGet hs (% joined w))))
              (set joined (+ joined 1))
            })
          (let ((h (__proc_spawn f i)))
            (if (< i w)
              (vecPush hs h)
              (vecSet hs (% i w) h)))
        })
      ; The drain continues the submission count left by the fill loop.
      ; Ring length is only its capacity, not the number submitted.
      (while (< joined n)
        {
          (vecPush out (__proc_join (vecGet hs (% joined w))))
          (set joined (+ joined 1))
        })
      (vecFree hs)
      out
    }))

; Join one `__proc_spawn` handle WITHOUT re-raising: `Ok` the thunk's
; word when the child exits 0, `Err` the child's wait status otherwise.
; `cell` is a live writable word owned by the calling pool. Every join
; overwrites it before returning, so one cell serves all sequential joins
; in that pool. It is never shared between concurrent pool invocations.
;
; PRIVATE, for `parRunWord`'s reason: the lossy-per-slot form is needed
; in one place, so it lives there, and a caller who wants the pool
; calls `parMapWordsChecked`.
(:: parJoinChecked (-> Spawn Int (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (parJoinChecked h cell)
  (let ((w (__proc_join_nr h cell)))
    (let ((st (memGetWord cell 0)))
      (if (== st 0)
        (Ok w)
        (Err (mkError st "parallel slot trapped"))))))

; Spawn `f` on `i` inside a recovery point of its own, so a refused
; spawn comes back here as a status rather than raising past the pool
; (MM-PAR-7's 78). Answers the handle's word, or the status: 78 when the
; kernel refused the fork or the handle table was full, 70 when the
; handle's page could not be mapped. The spawn is all the extent does,
; and the `region` around it takes back its one allocation, the mark.
(:: parSpawn (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
(fn (parSpawn f i)
  (region r
    (__axiom_recover
      __axiom_arena_mark
      (lambda (x) (parWordOf (__proc_spawn f i))))))

; Whether `parSpawn` answered a handle: a handle's word is its slot's
; generation, at least 1, times 2^16 plus the slot, and a status is
; below 256.
(:: parSpawned (-> Int Bool))
(fn (parSpawned hw)
  (>= hw 65536))

; A spawn handle as the word the recovery point can answer, and back.
; The way back makes a `Spawn` out of a word, so the word must be one
; `parSpawn` answered and `parSpawned` accepted: a `cast` to `Spawn` is
; not refused as an unsafe operation today (R-C6's limit), so that
; obligation is this module's, and this is its one such cast.
(:: parWordOf (-> Spawn Int))
(fn (parWordOf h)
  (cast Int h))

(:: parHandleOf (-> Int Spawn))
(fn (parHandleOf hw)
  (cast Spawn hw))

; SIGKILL a running slot's child, through the pid its handle names. A
; child that has already exited is a zombie, and the signal changes
; nothing about it.
(:: parKill (-> Spawn Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
(fn (parKill h)
  (match (sysKill (__spawn_pid h) 9)
    ((Ok z) 0)
    ((Err e) 0)))

; Join a slot `parKill` just signalled: `Ok` its word when it had
; finished first, and otherwise `Err` its wait status, which is 137.
(:: parJoinKilled (-> Spawn Int (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (parJoinKilled h cell)
  (let ((w (__proc_join_nr h cell)))
    (let ((st (memGetWord cell 0)))
      (if (== st 0)
        (Ok w)
        (Err (mkError st "parallel slot killed: a later spawn was refused"))))))

; Run `f i` for every `i` in `0 .. n`, at most `width` at once,
; answering one `Result` per slot in SUBMIT order: `Ok` the thunk's
; word, `Err` the wait status of a slot whose thunk trapped.
;
; A SPAWN THAT IS REFUSED stops the pool. Its slot and every slot after
; it answer `Err` with the refusal's status - 78 when the kernel refused
; the fork or the handle table was full, 70 when no page could be mapped
; for the handle - and never run. Every slot still running is SIGKILLed
; and joined, in submit order, and answers what its join saw: `Ok` its
; word if it finished first, otherwise `Err` 137. So no child outlives
; the call, and the scratch cell and ring are released on this path
; too.
;
; The loop is `parMapWords`'s, join for join - the width clamp, the
; block-before-spawning, the drain that continues `joined` - and the
; comments there are not repeated here. What differs is the join, the
; non-raising one, so a trapped slot is an `Err` in the answer and not
; the end of the program, and the spawn, which answers its refusal.
(pub :: parMapWordsChecked (-> (-> Int Int) Int Int (Vec (Result Int Error))))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (parMapWordsChecked f n width)
  (let (
    (w (if (< width 1)
      1
      width))
    (hs (vecWithCapacity
      (if (< n w)
        n
        w)))
    (out vecNew)
    (cell (memAlloc 8))
    (mut joined 0)
    (mut next 0)
    (mut refused 0)
  )
    {
      (__retain cell)
      (while (&& (< next n) (== refused 0))
        {
          (while (>= (- next joined) w)
            {
              (vecPush out (parJoinChecked (vecGet hs (% joined w)) cell))
              (set joined (+ joined 1))
            })
          (let ((hw (parSpawn f next)))
            (if (parSpawned hw)
              {
                (let ((h (parHandleOf hw)))
                  (if (< next w)
                    (vecPush hs h)
                    (vecSet hs (% next w) h)))
                (set next (+ next 1))
              }
              (set refused hw)))
        })
      (if (== refused 0)
        (while (< joined n)
          {
            (vecPush out (parJoinChecked (vecGet hs (% joined w)) cell))
            (set joined (+ joined 1))
          })
        {
          (for k in joined..next
            (parKill (vecGet hs (% k w))))
          (while (< joined next)
            {
              (vecPush out (parJoinKilled (vecGet hs (% joined w)) cell))
              (set joined (+ joined 1))
            })
          (for k in next..n
            (vecPush out (Err (mkError refused "parallel slot not started: its spawn was refused"))))
        })
      (vecFree hs)
      (__release cell)
      out
    }))

; A NULL-terminated array of char* from a Vec of `String`, which is the
; shape `execve` and `posix_spawn` both take.
;
; Every element goes through `strDup`, and that is not defensive
; copying - it is required. `strCStr` is `strData` and nothing more
; (Str.ax), so it is NUL-terminated only because every `String` that
; module BUILDS is. A `strSlice` is not built, it is `strWrap` over
; somebody else's buffer, so its bytes run on into whatever follows and
; the kernel reads a path or an argument that does not end where the
; `String` does. `tests/stdlib/300-process.ax` records the same rule for
; the same reason on its own argv helper, and
; `tests/stdlib/476-par-pool.ax` term 2 is the case that catches it.
(pub :: parArgvVector (-> (Vec String) Int))
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
(pub fn (parArgvVector argv)
  (let ((n (vecLen argv)))
    (let ((v (memAlloc (* 8 (+ n 1)))))
      {
        (for i in 0..n
          (memSetWord v i (strCStr (strDup (vecGetStr argv i)))))
        (memSetWord v n 0)
        v
      })))

; Run one argv - element 0 is the program, looked up on `PATH` the way
; `sysRunPath` does it.
;
; THIS ANSWERS A `Result` AND NOT A SENTINEL, and `Job.jobSubmitArgv`
; did not. That is not a gratuitous difference: `sysRunPath` has
; answered `(Result Int Error)` since 0.3.4, `Job` immediately re-encoded
; it back into an Int, and `compat/SENTINELS` counts exactly that kind of
; re-encoding on a PUBLIC name. The word form is still needed - only a
; word crosses a join - but it is needed in ONE place, so it lives there
; and is private (`parRunWord` below) rather than being this module's
; public surface.
;
; An EMPTY argv is `Err ENOENT` without spawning anything. There is no
; program to run, and "the program never ran" is exactly what that
; means.
(pub :: parRunOne (-> (Vec String) (Result Int Error)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(unsafe)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (parRunOne argv)
  (if (== (vecLen argv) 0)
    (Err (mkError 2 "parRunOne: an empty argv names no program"))
    (sysRunPath (strDup (vecGetStr argv 0)) (parArgvVector argv) sysEnvp)))

; `parRunOne` collapsed into the one word a join can carry, which is
; where `sysRun`'s sign convention gets re-established:
;
;   >= 0   the child's exit code
;   128+n  the child was killed by signal n
;   < 0    the program NEVER RAN, as a negated errno
;
; MATCH, not `unwrapOr`. The sign convention carries "the program never
; ran" in the same Int as an exit code, so a mechanical unwrap would
; destroy the distinction without the checker noticing; the arms cannot.
; That is the argument `Job.ax` made after `302-job` HUNG rather than
; failed, and it is inherited here rather than rediscovered.
;
; PRIVATE, deliberately. A caller who wants the failure in the type calls
; `parRunOne`; a caller who wants a pool calls `parRunAll` and reads the
; convention its header states. Nothing needs the lossy form by name.
(:: parRunWord (-> (Vec String) Int))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(fn (parRunWord argv)
  (match (parRunOne argv)
    ((Ok c) c)
    ((Err e) (- 0 (errCode e)))))

; Run every command in `cmds` at up to `width` at once, answering their
; exit codes in the order they appear in `cmds`.
;
; `cmds` is a Vec of Vecs: each inner Vec is an argv whose element 0 is
; the program, exactly as `sysRunPath` expects. This is the shape a gate
; script wants - a list of cases, a width, and an answer per case in the
; order it was listed - and it is `Job.jobRunAll`'s signature and
; contract unchanged, which is what makes the replacement a drop-in.
;
; The lookup of element 0 happens in the CHILD here, where `Job` did it
; in the parent. The observable contract is the same either way: a
; missing program answers `-ENOENT` in its own slot and occupies that
; slot, because `sysRunPath` resolves the name BEFORE it spawns and
; answers `Err` without ever exec'ing. What changed is only which
; process pays for the `PATH` walk.
(pub :: parRunAll (-> (Vec (Vec String)) Int (Vec Int)))
;@axiom:effect(io)
;@axiom:effect(spawn)
;@axiom:effect(block)
(pub fn (parRunAll cmds width)
  (parMapWords
    (lambda (i) (parRunWord (vecGet cmds i)))
    (vecLen cmds)
    width))
